Terry et. al. Quick Comment:

Science -- of which statistics is a part -- is a human endeavor that is
neither so objective nor so "scientifically based" as we purport. Models go
in and out of fashion, hopefully driven by "facts" and observation of
Nature, but not always. As a kid, I was taught that continents were
immovable (something like plate tectonics had been proposed 50 years
previously) and that the noble gases were inert (xenon tetrafluoride was a
high school experiment).  And there is still hot debate on terrestrial/extra
terrestrial origins for mass extinctions.

So taboos, legends, and folklore are just as much a part of our science as
others. I strongly doubt that there is "no basis for the taboos," although
the "wisdom" from which they may have originated may be flawed or outdated.
No matter, our job is to move things "forward". As in all matters, it just
takes awhile. As you alluded, when large gobs of money are involved, it can
be even more difficult. But if a  case can be clearly and convincingly made,
I'm sure FDA and the pharmaceuticals will respond. 

