From Mauricio.Ortiz at noaa.gov  Tue Jun  1 18:44:21 1999
From: Mauricio.Ortiz at noaa.gov (Mauricio Ortiz)
Date: Tue, 01 Jun 1999 14:44:21 -0400
Subject: [S] Type III errors and LSMeans. Replay from Original poster
Message-ID: <0280137542A05004*/c=US/admd=ATTMAIL/prmd=GOV+NOAA/o=CCNMFS/s=Ortiz/g=Mauricio/@MHS>

Hi

First I will thank to all the statisticians and scientist that respond to my
request of Type III errors and LSmeans.
I must clarify that I am a fisheries biologist with some statistical
background, but not a formal Statistician.  Howe-
ver I am very CONCERN with my "perception" of type III error, lsmeans, and
the usefulness of such statistics..

In general the response from Dr. Brian Ripley suggest that type III error
are non-use, and (I conclude) test based
on this type of Sum of Squares are non-useful also.    Comments in the
article from Dr. Wanables (Exegeses on Linear Models)
also suggest type III error are non-useful and misleading as ignoring
interactions effects between main effects.

Perhaps it will be better if I state in WHAT TYPE of models (data) I have
been using, evaluating type III errors and LSMeans, as to
set a common ground for discussion     I deal mostly with fisheries data,
where balance designs are only in books.  Most common we
have highly unbalance designs, samples (observations) collected from
multiple sources without a truly Experimental Design,  The usual
quantifiable variables are total catch (kgs, or number fish), and some
indicator of effort (hours, boats #, # hooks per set etc). we estimate
a Catch Per Unit of Effort (CPUE) and then try to explained Yearly trends of
CPUE based on auxiliary information such as spatio (area, depth)
time (season, month...) type of fishing gear, commercial vrs recreational,
etc. factors.   In this frame, is normal to have "empty cells" in the model
matrix.  Again the main objective is to extract the Yearly trend from the
observed CPUE, and then use this yearly trend (or INDEX) in age structured
or other population models, where the INDEX is some how the modulator of
the Fish Stock size.

My "assumptions" of type III SS is that they were independent of the order
of the effects in the model,  and it is common that type I SS is different
(and test of factor significance) depending upon the order or factors in the
model.
Second I selected LSMean for the Year effect as the corrected estimated
yearly mean assuming that the rest of factors are "average".  This is
analogous to the "mean treatment effect".
Another characteristic of fisheries data, is that they are usually a large
number of observations ( ie. about thousands to 10K), therefore I had found
that most test are "always significant".   And only with a Deviance
analysis,( including percent of deviance)  is possible to find the relative
importance of factors to the overall model in terms of explaining the
observed variability.

I am very interested in this topic, I am hope this discussion will continue,
I have definitely learn a great deal but As always The more you know the
More questions are generated.

Mauricio Ortiz
Fishery Biologist
NOAA/NMFS Miami lab
