From Bill.Venables at cmis.CSIRO.AU  Wed Jun  2 12:44:09 1999
From: Bill.Venables at cmis.CSIRO.AU (Venables, Bill (CMIS, Cleveland))
Date: Wed, 02 Jun 1999 22:44:09 +1000
Subject: RE: [S] Type III
Message-ID: <D16979FB73F3D11195510000F8030FB90D0A69@roper.qld.cmis.csiro.au>

(My comments on Terry's thoughtful piece below will be prefixed
by ***.)

-----Original Message-----
From: Therneau, Terry M., Ph.D.
To: Bill.Venables at cmis.csiro.au
Cc: s-news at wubios.wustl.edu; Therneau, Terry M., Ph.D.
Sent: 6/1/99 12:40 PM
Subject: [S] Type III

The BMDP manual of many years ago had a nice explanation.  Lacking
subscripts, let's assume a 2 way table of the following structure:

        A1      A2      A3      A4
        B1      B2      B3      B4
        C1      C2      C3      C4

Assume no empty cells, then there are 12 estimable parameters.  The
type III (Yates weighted means) hypothesis is

rows: .25(a1+a2+a3+a4) = .25(b1+b2+b3+b4) = .25(c1+c2+c3+c4)

That is, the average row effects are identical.  I think this hypothesis
makes sense even without perfect additivity.  (After all, perfect 
additivity only exists in simulations: I have real data).  If I know 
something about the population of interest, as opposed to the data set, I 
might argue for different column weights: public health questions are 
rational ones.

 *** Exactly.  If one of he column classes is persistently going to be
 *** 97% of the population and the other three 1% each, then there is
 *** every chance that these equally weighted row effects are not at all
 *** interesting and testing any hypothesis on them ultimately misleading.

 *** If you retain non-additivity then you have essentially a single 
 *** classification and no one would argue that testing any set of contrasts
 *** is letitimate and under some circumstances informative, but which 
 *** hypothesis is being tested should always be clear and upfront to the
 *** user, not masked by the curious language of "Type III" sums of squares.
 
I also see a lot of similarity between an argument for Yates' test and
for "intention to treat" analyses: some people take their drug, some not, 
we know there is differntial treatment effect ('interaction') but still
report the averaged coefficient.

 *** There is no doubt that under some circumstances estimates of marginal
 *** means and hypothesis tests on them are sensible things to do, but the
 *** user always has a duty to understand what hypothesis, precisely, is
 *** being tested.  Providing "Type III" sums of squares, with their vague
 *** promise of "testing an hypothesis on the main effects not dependent on
 *** the sample sizes and appropriate even when interactions are present" is
 *** just plain misleading and irresponsible.

 *** There is, of course, a good argument for providing better assistance to
 *** the user to test non-standard hypotheses on the mean vector, but that
is
 *** a separate issue.

   Now:
    1. Writing this hypothesis in terms of "main effects + interaction"
coefficients is only a recipe for confusion.  It reveals nothing and
obscures much.

 *** Having studied Analysis of Variance from Scheff\'e's book many years
ago
 *** I agree with your.  The means of the observations should always be the
 *** starting point, in my view.

        a. First problem: it leads to an algorithm that can be applied
when there are missing cells.  When there are missing cells the contrast
above isn't estimable, and the one the algorithm computes is hogwash.
(A rule of thumb for SAS-- if type III and type IV are different, both are
misguided).

        b. Second problem:  Apostles of the marginality creed think that
I'm doing something odd with iteractions:  several of the S-news responses
are in that camp.

 *** The essence of the "marginality creed" is really that testing
hypotheses
 *** founded on arbitrary choices or concealed assumptions is likely to lead
 *** to trouble.  If there are interactions present, the weights used to
define
 *** main effects are likely to be crucial to any interpretation.  Selecting

 *** equal weights for want of something better *is* arbitrary and dressing
it
 *** up in this silly language of "Type III" sums of squares is doing a
great
 *** job of occluding that fact.

   2. I have yet to see someone write the hypothesis in the rational
way, i.e., like above, and then argue that the test makes no sense.  You 
can argue that other tests may be better, and they may well be.  

 *** As an old, ex-educator, I'm always keen to further people's
experiences, 
 *** Terry.  :-)  More seriously, I would not argue that it makes no sense.
I
 *** would definitely argue, though, that more often than not it does, and
most
 *** users are unaware of that fact.  Making "Type III" the default would be
 *** definitely sub-optimal.

(I most 
often use sequential sums of squares myself).  Arguments that ___ is the 
"only" or "best" test are of course misguided, and like others I cringe at 
the enthronment of type III SS.)

 *** yes, indeed.

    3. To the extent that Splus has copied an algorithm rather than an
idea, we are in for a confusing future.  SAS has lots of good documentation,
but "The four types of estimable functions" is solidly on the negative
side of the ledger.

 *** Amen to all of that.  All sums of squares I have ever seen used are
 *** of the same type, sequential, but what is not usually made clear is 
 *** what are the two nested hypotheses involved.  Moreover dressing sums
 *** of squares up this way only encourages users to remain ignorant.  

