Message-ID: <41256784.0057848F.00@nts1.novartis.com>

Let X=1,2,3, or 4.
One can easily get the F-statistic and p-value associated with the type X sums
of squares from SAS and now S-Plus.
The hypothesis test associated with the type X sums of squares F-statistic is
not always very interesting, and Terry and Bill
have illustrated why this is so in a very nice way.
One should always write out (or at least be aware of) the corresponding
hypothesis test for each F-statistic (X=1,2,3, and 4)
for your particular experiment/data, and if it makes sense, then you should feel
free to use it in good health.
This "you should absolutely never do ______" dogma is not very productive.

The FDA will only take issue with you if you are using an F-statistic which
corresponds to a
hypothesis that is strange or has no relevance to your given experiement, data,
or drug.
The only way you can know this is to write out the hypotheses for each
F-statistic, and see for yourself.
As for the "FDA always wants ______" , this is not true (no matter how many
times people continue to say it).
You can freely use whatever F-statistic (X=1,2,3, and 4) you wish, provided that
the corresponding hypothesis test for that F-statistic is answers a relevant
scientific question.

Unfortunately, the documentation in the software packages is lacking, and some
users will invariably be led astray.
Fortunately we have folks like Venables and Ripley, Harrell, Therneau, and
others to fill the gap quite nicely.

In closing, how about some references from the literature?  Other than the
standard texts on linear models, I don't know
of any really good articles in the statistical literature on the pro's and con's
of the 4 different types of sums of squares ?
Surely there must be many.

Eric Gibson
