The BMDP manual of many years ago had a nice explanation.  Lacking
subscripts, let's assume a 2 way table of the following structure:

     A1   A2   A3   A4
     B1   B2   B3   B4
     C1   C2   C3   C4

Assume no empty cells, then there are 12 estimable parameters.  The
type III (Yates weighted means) hypothesis is

rows: .25(a1+a2+a3+a4) = .25(b1+b2+b3+b4) = .25(c1+c2+c3+c4)

That is, the average row effects are identical.  I think this hypothesis
makes sense even without perfect additivity.  (After all, perfect
additivity only exists in simulations: I have real data).  If I know
something about the population of interest, as opposed to the data set, I
might argue for different column weights: public health questions are
rational ones.

