From ecm at skew2.kellogg.nwu.edu  Wed Jun  2 17:08:20 1999
From: ecm at skew2.kellogg.nwu.edu
Date: Wed, 02 Jun 1999 12:08:20 -0500
Subject: [S] marginal terms and Tukey's ladder
Message-ID: <199906021708.MAA27754@skew2.kellogg.nwu.edu>

Dear Snews readers,

I have thoroughly enjoyed the discussion on type III sums of 
squares and the "Exegeses" paper.  I'd like to describe what seems 
to me as a contradiction in the regression literature on the 
closely related topic of including terms marginal to x^p, where p>1 
is an integer.  This topic is covered briefly in the Exegeses 
paper.  I'll first summarize the contradiction then give quotes 
from texts.  Many respected texts on regression make sweeping 
dismissals of non-hierarchical models with polynomial terms, yet 
many of these same books discuss transformations of predictor 
variables such as the square root, logarithm, and inverse.  If I'm 
reading them correctly, I can have terms such as x^.5 and x^-1 in a 
model without any other relatives of x, but I should never have 
have a model that has x^2 in it without an x term.  I am puzzled 
why these authors are so strict about including lower-order terms 
with the square transformation, but not with others.  What is 
sacred about integer powers greater than or equal to 2?  The 
problem seems to be that there is a distinction between a 
"polynomial" model and a model with "transformations." 

Here are some quotes:

Draper and Smith, 1998, pp. 267-271
They give two criteria for a "sensible" (well formulated) model.  
The first is that if we shift the origin of the X-space, the "form" 
of the model remains unchanged.  The second deals with invariance 
under rotations of the X-space.  They show that if you accept the 
first criteria, then only the highest-order terms can be deleted at 
any point in time (p. 268).  In their summary advice, they suggest 
"always apply rule 1" (p. 271).  The Exegeses paper also gives rule
1 as a reason to prefer hierarchical models.

First, if I understand their definition of the word "form," then 
the form of a model with log, sqrt, or inverse transformations is 
not invariant under changes in origin.  Second, the origin-shift
criterion is not relevant if I have a meaningful origin (e.g., a
scale with "ratio" measurement level or what Tukey calls "amounts" 
and "counts").  For example, it makes no sense to change the origin 
of a dollar amount, count, or length variable (particularly if 0 is 
included in my neighborhood of interest).  Third, a quadratic 
transformation without the linear term gives a parabola with the 
constraint that its min/max value be at x=0.  I can think of many 
situations where it makes sense to have the mean of y change 
monotonically with x.  If I add the x term I an no longer 
guaranteed a monotonic relationship.  

McCullagh and Nelder, 1989, page 89
``it is important that the final model or models should make sense 
physically:  at a minimum, this usually means that interactions 
should not be included without main effects nor higher-degree 
polynomial terms without their lower-degree relatives.'' 

At least these authors use the word "usually." 

Mosteller and Tukey (1977) seem to take a much different (and bold) 
position on the matter.  Ch 4 describes their "ladder of 
re-expressions" (x^p where p in {-3,-2,-1,-1/2,#,1/2,1,2,3}), which 
are designed to straighten out the relationship between x and y.  
As far as I can tell, they do not give any special consideration to 
powers 2 and 3.

In summary, I think that regression texts should not make sweeping 
dismissals of non-hierarchical models with power terms.  While 
there are certainly good reasons to have a hierarchical model 
(e.g., Draper and Smith's two rules), there are also good reasons 
not to have a hierarchical models, which are not usually covered, 
such as the measurement level of the variable (ratio-amount-count 
versus others), if 0 is in the neighborhood of interest, and 
whether the relationship should be monotonic.  It seems like more 
balanced discussion should be included on this subject.  IMO,
Mosteller and Tukey have great discussion on this subject.  I find
it strange that subsequent texts don't build on their work (e.g.,
Draper and Smith (1998) and Neter, et al. (1996) don't even
reference Mosteller and Tukey).

Thank you,

Ed Malthouse

