Subject: [S] Summary of Replies: S+ vs Matlab
Message-ID: <3756C202.7BA33654@worldnet.att.net>

My original posting was:

> I have been doing some calculations with extremely large data sets.
> I have heard that Matlab is better at handling large data sets than
S+.
> I have several questions regarding Matlab and S+
>  Is Matlab really better than S+ for very large data sets?
>    Is Matlab an open extensible language like S+?
>    Is Matlab object oriented?
>    Is there a users group or mail group for Matlab?
>    Is the C/Fortran interface good in Matlab?

Dr K.M. Briggs points out an excellent collection of user reviews at:
http://www.informatik.uni-frankfurt.de/~stst/testanw.html#Matlab
Unfortunately, S+ is not directly compared to Matlab in these reviews.

Richard Johnson writes:
Matlab has become better at coping with large data sets with recent
versions. The current version, V5.3, is much, much better than V4.2.
Matlab has similar openness and extensibility to S+.
The graphics in Matlab are clearly object oriented. The orientation of
other features is a bit murky.
The news group is comp.soft-sys.matlab.
There are C and Fortran interfaces. The C interface, at least, allows
either to be the client. I haven't used either.
You didn't ask about statistical routines. S+ is clearly ahead in this
department, but some of us are moving Matlab along.

Francisco Cribari points out R:
 http://www.de.ufpe.br/~cribari/r.ps
The main thing I got from this comparison is that R is much better at
programming loops
than S. The similar problem of memory allocation, (S does not free
memory at each loop iteration but creates a persistent object on the
hard drive and in memory!), is not specifically dealt with. I am almost
tempted to try a memory test on R to see if it handles memory better. I
wish the programmers at Lucent and Mathsoft would hack this code so that
the S+ product could not be beaten by freeware in simple timing test.

Clark Gayloard has done loop test of Splus 4.5, Splus 5.0, Mathematica
and Matlab. He finds that Splus 4.5 and Matlab are about the same. Splus
5.0 is much slower ??!! It looks like the programers at Lucent are more
concerned with linguistic sugar than performance. He also states that S+
and Matlab are both superior to Mathematica for numerical work. He also
says that there were many licensing problems with Matlab.

Osman Buyukisik suggest freeware replacements of S - R and Matlab -
ocatave or scilab
He says they all have C/C++/Fortram interfaces.

Douglas Bates says ocatave is available at:
  http://www.che.wisc.edu/octave/

Stefan Steinhaus has written an excellent comparison of 11 separate math
packages. He does speed test, in addition to many other test, and S+ 4.5
compares favorably to all other programs. On most test Matlab 5.3 is
slightly faster, but on some test S+ 4.5 is slightly faster.  For the
most part there is no significant difference between S+4.5 and Matlab
5.3!!!!!!!!! Also in Stefans work S+ is faster than R! Stefans work is
available at
http://www.informatik.uni-frankfurt.de/~stst/ncrunch/index.html

Michael Wolf points out that in Stefan's determinant test, Stefan has
chosen a very inefficient method for calculating determinants, so
Stefans speed test are biased against S+.

Andrew Sinclair writes:
> In my humble experience Matlab is a lot faster
> for handling large datasets.  However it does have fewer
> statistical functions so whether using it will save you time depends
> on how advanced the statistical analysis you want to do is.
> The development environment is better (IMHO) than that for Splus
> for Windows, and it's nice that (for example) when you hit CTRL-C
> it comes back immediately rather than taking ages.
>
> The answers to your last three questions are yes ,yes, and yes.

Jim Holtman writes:
 I am processing matrices with 9 colums and 2,000,000 rows on my
SPLUS system without any problems.
SPLUS is CPU bound if there is enough memory.  Your array has 10M
entries
that are at least 8 bytes in length, giving a size of 80MB.  If multiple

copies are made during the processing, this is what is causing your
problems.

Simon Westwood writes:
> As for being better at handling large data sets, I would not like to
comment
> on that.  I believe it is slightly better than S-Plus at memory
management
> and for loops are alot faster to execute.  In some cases S-Plus is
faster,
> others MATLAB.
> MATLAB is very much like S-Plus but more mathematically orientated.
> It is, to an extend, object oriented (depending on how you define OO)
> and it as open to modification as S-Plus.  There are also numerous
toolboxes
> available, similar to the S-Plus libraries, however many are classed
as
> commercial software.

The conclusion I have come to, is that MATLAB or R might be more
effective at avoiding disk writes and paging because they uses memory
more efficiently, but S+ is about as fast as all other alternatives if
your computer has enough RAM.

Thanks to all have written, I hope I have not forgotten anybody.

Clark Sims

