Keith,

This maybe a difficult model to fit using the glm, ms or nlminb functions since
the model itself is not constrained to yield predicted values on the unit
interval. However, one way to accomplish this is to maximize the log-likelihood
using a nonlinear programming routine, such as the Nelder-Mead simplex search
procedure for function minimization. The Splus function containing the objective
function may look something like the following pseudo code:

programming statements....

P <- B0 + B1 * exp (- days/B3)
log.likelihood<- r*log(P)+(n-r)log(1-P)

# to maximize the likelihood, minimize the negative
# make the objective function value large when P>1 or P<0

if 0<= P <=1 then obj <- -log.likelihood
     else obj <- 10^38

obj
}
where there are r "successes" in n trials. Here, a constraint has been imposed
that the predicted probability is between 0 and 1. The function nlminb could
constrain B0 and B1 to be within 0 and 1, but that wouldn't guarantee that P
would fall within these limits.

How do you do this in Splus? On 1/28/99, Paul Gilbert wrote:

There is a version of the Nelder-Mead algorithm (essentially S from C from Press
et al "Numerical Recipes in C") included with my time series library "DSE"
available at <http://www.bank-banque-canada.ca/pgilbert>

Hope this helps.

Max

