Subject: [S] ms() for vector arguments
Message-ID: <Pine.HPP.4.10.9906071149180.23389-100000@sloth.stat.cmu.edu>

If the function's argument and parameters are vectors with indefinite
length,
rather than just single values, how may I use ms to find the optimizers?

For example:

# RET, RET.0: data vector of length n 
# a0, a1: parameter vector of length n
# RHOS: parameter (correlation) matrix of dimensionality (n x n)
# n may change for different datasets.
loglik.arch.m <- function(RET, RET.0, a0, a1, RHOS){
  sigma <- sqrt(a0 + a1 * RET.0^2)

  H  <- sigma %o% sigma * RHOS

  -( t(RET) %*% solve(H) %*% RET + (1/2)*log(prod(eigen(H)$values)) )
}

foo <- 
data.frame(today=cbind(rnorm(10),rnorm(10)),yesterday=cbind(rnorm(10),rnorm(10)))

dork.ms <- ms(~-loglik.arch.m(today,yesterday,aa,bb,
rr),start=list(aa=c(0.0002,0.0002),bb=c(0.1,0.1),rr=diag(2)),data=foo)

#These two lines above do not work because "today" is not a variable name
#for the data frame.

Usually the function to miniimize has numerical, rather than vector
arguments.

