In the definition of the function "loglik.arch.m.2", I meant SIGMA is
a vector of length 2. Instead ms() computes for the whole data frame
and SIGMA's length is 20 (see the following example). It looks that
RET1 in the example is taken to be a vector, rather than a single value 
(current). How to revise so that SIGMA is changed over each row of the
data.frame?

What ms() does here seems conter-intuitive.

loglik.arch.m.2 <- function( RET1, RET2, RET1.0, RET2.0, a10, a20, a11,
a21, rho12 ){
  SIGMA <- sqrt(c(a10+a11*RET1.0*RET1.0, a20+a21*RET2.0*RET2.0))
  # SIGMA should always have length 2; but it has length 20 in the example
  # below

  print(length(SIGMA))
  RHOS <- matrix(c(1,rho12,rho12,1),2,2)
 
  H <- SIGMA %o% SIGMA * RHOS
  print(H)
  RET <- c(RET1, RET2)
  -( t(RET) %*% solve(H) %*% RET + (1/2)*log(prod(eigen(H)$values)) )
}

foo<-data.frame(today.1=C.1998[-1], today.2=DM.1998[-1],
yesterday.1=C.1998[-261], yesterday.2=DM.1998[-261])
