Current Index to Statistics Publishes New Edition and Goes On-Line

[apologies for cross-posting]

CURRENT INDEX TO STATISTICS PUBLISHES NEW EDITION AND GOES ON-LINE

The Current Index to Statistics is the premier bibliographic reference
for the statistics literature.  The 23rd edition of the print volume,
containing about 9700 items - primarily articles, books and reviews
appearing in 1997 -  is currently in the mail to subscribers.  Release
7 of the CIS Extended Database on CD-ROM was also recently mailed,
containing cumulative entries from the entire series of print volumes
as well as extended earlier coverage that does not appear in print, for
a total of about 197,500 records.  The Print Volumes and licenses for
the Extended Database are sold through the ASA and IMS offices.
Information about purchase of both versions can be found at
http://www.statindex.org.

NEW WEB SERVER FOR CIS

CIS has now added a new service for its university customers: a
centralized server for Web access to the CIS database.  The server was
established in response to demand from libraries and other key users who
wanted the benefits of access through a Web interface without having to
find the personnel and computer resources to set it up themselves.  More
information on the new server can be found at the URL
http://query.statindex.org/.  

CURRENT INDEX TO STATISTICS EDITOR SEARCH

The Current Index to Statistics Management Committee is searching for a
new Abstracting Editor to replace Professor Klaus Hinkelmann of Virginia
Tech.  Professor Hinkelmann will complete his term as CIS editor when
abstracting for 1999 publications is completed in mid-2000.  The
Abstracting Editor prepares for and supervises entry of bibliographic
records into the database, coordinates the efforts of volunteer
abstractors (Contributing Editors) for journals outside the core set,
and selects consistent and scientifically meaningful and appropriate
keywords to be used in bibliographic searches.  CIS is in an exciting
period of development including the opening of a Web server and
modernization and systematization of our operations in several areas.
We welcome suggestions and nominations for possible editors who could
take part in further development of this important resource for the
Statistics profession.

