From: WebsterR at landcare.cri.nz (Ray Webster)
Date: Wed, 09 Jun 1999 16:45:48 +1200
Subject: [S] Summary: drop-out in repeated measures data
Message-ID: <s75e9a63.065@landcare.cri.nz>

Thanks to those who responded to my question concerning what was
available in S-Plus for modelling longitudinal data when subjects drop out
(die) before the end of the study.  In our study there were three treatment
groups, and the response variable was proportion of each period an
animal performed a particular behaviour.  Each animal was observed
either until the end of the study or until its death (the first deaths came
half way through the study).  

Charles Berry said that the missing values due to the drop-out were
"missing at random" (MAR) for this data as they only depended on what
was observed prior to death.  He said that in this case lme could be used
for analysis as it only assumes MAR, and not "missing completely at
random" (MCAR).

Frank Harrell said: "Take a look at the rm.impute function in the Design
library. This function is still under development and has not yet been
validated, but soon it will be useful for the kinds of problems
you are addressing."

Orna Intrator pointed to some literature, of which there is now quite a lot. 
However, to my knowledge Diggle and Kenward's (1994) model available
in Oswald remains the only one specifically designed for modelling
drop-out in longitudinal data available in S-Plus.  It's a very computationally
intensive model, and there can be great difficulty in achieving
convergence.

A couple of people suggested using survival analysis, but the problem is
more complicated as we're interested in analysing the longitudinal
response of the subjects and not only the drop-out times.

thanks again

Ray Webster

