From: Max_Kuhn at ms.bd.com
Date: Thu, 10 Jun 1999 15:06:16 -0400
Subject: Re: [S] Pooled Adjacent Violator Algorithm
In-Reply-To: <059526454901D211B07F00A0C9B3BC751A4188@mail2.pica.army.mil>
Message-ID: <8525678C.00690F50.00@7crmta_md.ms.bd.com>

From: Max Kuhn@BDX on 06/10/99 03:06 PM

John,

Here is code I lifted from someone else's (non S-plus) program:

 abers<-function(events, trials)
{
     k <- length(events)
     p <- matrix(0, k, 1)
     pp <- matrix(0, k, k)
     for(i in 1:k) {
          sum1 <- 0
          sum2 <- 0
          for(j in i:k) {
               sum1 <- sum1 + events[j]
               sum2 <- sum2 + trials[j]
               pp[i, j] <- sum1/sum2
          }
          temp <- as.matrix(pp[(1:i), (i:k)])
          p[i] <- ifelse(i > 1, max(apply(temp, 1, min)), min(temp))
     }
     cbind(events/trials, p)
}

p is the abers estimate of the sample proportion under order restrictions. It
assumes the data are in sequential order. I'm sure it can be programmed more
efficiently. There are k*(k+1)/2 executions of the loop, where k is the number
of groups. It runs pretty quick on my machine (v4.5) for the example below.

Here are some data from Morgan (1992, pg. 305):

> n<-rep(6,9)
> r<-c(0,0,1,0,2,4,4,6,5)
> abers(r,n)
          [,1]       [,2]
[1,] 0.0000000 0.00000000
[2,] 0.0000000 0.00000000
[3,] 0.1666667 0.08333333
[4,] 0.0000000 0.08333333
[5,] 0.3333333 0.33333333
[6,] 0.6666667 0.66666667
[7,] 0.6666667 0.66666667
[8,] 1.0000000 0.91666667
[9,] 0.8333333 0.91666667
>

Enjoy-

Max

