Visualizing Data:  Building Models for Data
by William S. Cleveland
A Two-Day Short Course
Sponsored by the LearnSTAT program of the ASA
Seattle, Washington July 19-20
Cost: $800.00
Place: University of Washington

COURSE CONTENT
Powerful new methods of data visualization, together with modern computing
environments, have combined to produce a new frontier of data analysis.  The
course will present visualization tools that provide deep insight into the
structure of data.  There are graphical tools such as coplots, level plots,
and banking to 45 degrees.  There are fitting tools such as loess and
bisquare.  Some tools are old standards.  One example is q-q plots.  Many
tools are new, the leading edge of approaches to data visualization.  One
example is Trellis
display, a framework for analyzing multivariable data; Trellis provides the
most powerful mechanism yet developed for understanding interactions in
studies of how a response depends on explanatory variables.

But the material in the course is much more than a compendium of useful
tools.  It conveys an approach to data analysis that stresses the use of
visualization to thoroughly study the structure of data and to check the
validity of statistical models fitted to data.  The result is a vast
increase in what can be learned from data.  The course demonstrates this by
reanalyzing many data sets from the scientific literature, revealing missed
effects and inappropriate models fitted to data.

WHO SHOULD ATTEND
The course is intended for anyone who has data to analyze.  The
prerequisites are familiarity with basic statistics and the least-squares
method of fitting equations to data.  For example, an introductory course in
statistics that included the fundamentals of regression analysis would be
sufficient.

FEE & MATERIALS
The tuition fee is $800.  Participants will receive course materials,
breakfast and lunch for both days.  

INSTRUCTOR
William S. Cleveland is a leading researcher in statistical methods. His
previous writings on visualization, many papers and three books, have
enjoyed a large audience.  Tools introduced in these writings are widely
used throughout the scientific community.  For more information on Dr.
Cleveland, see cm.bell-labs.com/stat/wsc.

SEATTLE
