From lscudieri at ultracom.net  Sat Jun 12 16:47:26 1999
From: lscudieri at ultracom.net (Lorraine Scudieri)
Date: Sat, 12 Jun 1999 12:47:26 -0400
Subject: [S] Duplicate observations
Message-ID: <37628F1E.77AFBEC5@ultracom.net>

My thanks to all of you who responded to my question regarding the
ability to identify unique observations in a data set and
count the number of occurrences of each unique observation when the
number of observations is very large in comparison to
the number of variables.  The general solution is to collapse the
variables in each observation to one character string, then
identify and count the unique observations.  The code I used was derived
from Todd Taylor's response, as follows.

  if your data are in 'my.data':

     one.var <- apply(my.data, 1, paste, collapse=",")

  Then evaluate duplicates however you like:

     is.duped   <- duplicated(one.var)
     unique.obs <- one.var[!is.duped]

  or

     data.split <- split(one.var, one.var)
     unique.obs <- names(data.split)
     n.of.each  <- unlist(lapply(data.split, length))

    Although my implementation was successful for small cases, when I
applied it to 6^6 observations with 6 variables, the
program ran for 24 hours when I finally killed it (it died
unpleasantly).  My program first generates the 6^6 observations, each
variable is one digit, then the above code follows.  I realize that 6^6
observations is large, but, what is taking so long?  Does
anyone have any insight into what is happening?  There is plenty of
memory; there are no loops; the program was apparently
chugging along quite happily when I killed it.   I have no concept of
how long something like this should take.

    Again, thanks to all of you who replied.  It is nice to know that
there are people out there I can count on for help.

Lorraine

