From acebrian at posta.unizar.es  Sun Jun 13 05:56:30 1999
From: acebrian at posta.unizar.es
Date: Sun, 13 Jun 1999 07:56:30 +0200
Subject: [S] Summary exponential error
Message-ID: <3.0.5.32.19990613075630.007b7d70@posta.unizar.es>

 Thank you to Ori Davidov ,     Albyn Jones and Rick Chappell for answering my
question about exponential error

''I want to fit a general linear model with exponential error. I know that
if I use family=Gamma  with scale parameter=1, I will get an exponential
error, but how can I force the scale parameter to be 1, using the glm
funtion? is there any other possibility?

I have another doubt, which are the differences between 'glm' and 'glim'
functions?
''

Summary of their answers

- The scale parameter is irrelevant to GLMs fitted via iteratively
reweighted least squares.  If you are interested
in estimating the scale parameter, you do it the same way that you estimate
residual standard error in ordinary least squares: using the residuals after
the model is fit.

Note that this does not yield the joint maximum likelihood estimators of the
coefficients and the scale parameters (although it is usually close).  If
you want to specify a certain member of the gamma family you might look in
the survival analysis routines'

To get information about the scale (dispersion) parameter 

        gamma.fit <- glm(formula = ti ~ x, family = Gamma(link = 
                inverse))
        expreg.fit <- summary(gamma.fit, dispersion = 1)

obviously ti and x are your times and covariates respectively. 

