From macq at llnl.gov  Tue Jun 15 16:45:12 1999
From: macq at llnl.gov (Don MacQueen)
Date: Tue, 15 Jun 1999 09:45:12 -0700
Subject: [S] directing ... to different places
Message-ID: <v03130303b38c2a597321@[128.115.153.6]>

In Splus 5.1, unix, I'm developing a plot method for the case in which x is
of class timeDate (Mathsoft provides one for timeSeries but not timeDate).
Here's what I've got so far.

-----------------------------------------------------------
setMethod("plot", signature(x="timeDate"),
function(x, y, ...)  invisible(plot.timeDate(x, y, ...))
)

plot.timeDate <- function(x,y,...) {
  xrng <- range(x,na.rm=T)
  x <- as(x,'numeric')
  plot(x,y,xaxt='n',...)
  x.axis <- axis.time(xrng)
  x.axis$scale$plot.coordinates <- as.numeric(x.axis$scale$user.coordinates)
  axis.render(1,ticks=x.axis$ticks,labels=x.axis$labels,breaks=x.axis$breaks,
              scale=x.axis$scale)
  invisible(x.axis)
}
-----------------------------------------------------------

However, the axis.time() function has optional arguments. I would like to
use the ... argument in plot.timeDate() to pass optional arguments to both
plot() and to axis.time(). I thought I had a way to do this, by creating a
personal version of axis.time() which has the ... argument. Like this:

    x.axis <- axis.time(xrng,...)

This worked until I discovered that plot() crashes when ... includes an
object of class timeSpan.

The error message is
  Problem in FUN(x = x, y = y, min.span = ..1, xlab = xlab, ylab = ylab):
  internal error: can't convert class "timeSpan" for S Version 2 object
The implication is that plot() still uses an S Version 2 function, which
doesn't know what to do with newer S version 4 classes.

I think there may be a solution involving do.call(), and I'm working on it.
In the meantime, So I would appreciate suggestions for how to use ... and
have the optional arguments be passed to the correct function. A quick and
dirty way is to include all of the optional axis.time() arguments in
plot.timeDate(), and use ... in plot() only.

Thanks
-Don

[By the way, axis.time() fails when one of its arguments, nobreaks, is T.]

[why not use the method for timeSeries you ask?
 1) Look at par()usr. The x axis is not scaled in user units.
 2) The plot style defaults are nowhere near what I need for my ordinary work
 3) My data has multiple measurements on the same date and at the same
time. The plot method for timeSeries crashes with such data.]

